VERDICT: REWORK
SNAPSHOT: 08e8539eb8b451f3508932ed396506d25e59906ab0c2fe9e0a50c85b11982372
ROUND: 1
SUMMARY: The Tor artifact itself is checked before installation, but unchecked dependencies can still be selected from the mutable Tor repository; constrain that selection before approving this candidate.

FINDINGS:

RT1 — Major — Mutable Tor repository can replace the executable dependency closure.
Location: sidecars/tor/install-tor.sh:24 (also :25–27; sidecars/tor/Dockerfile:167–170).
Affected properties: Constitution IV/VI, feature FR-015, THREAT-MODEL §2.6/§5.9.
Reachable attack path: A compromised deb.torproject.org archive publisher/signing key leaves the reviewed tor_0.4.9.12 .deb unchanged but adds a higher-version libevent-2.1-7 to its signed Packages index. The new installer asks APT to resolve/download Tor and its dependencies while both repositories are enabled at their default priorities. Only the Tor archive is checked against an independently reviewed hash; the unchecked library is then installed from the private cache by --no-download. Its maintainer scripts execute during the build and/or its code executes inside Tor, which sees this route's destinations and circuit state. Neither a valid Tor checksum nor offline installation prevents this path. This dependency-selection exposure existed in the old installation flow and is retained in the new installer; it is not claimed to have been introduced by the version bump. It directly affects the in-scope installer and its immutable-input guarantee, rather than an unrelated runtime subsystem.
Attempted falsification: Independently verified the actual signed amd64 and arm64 Tor metadata: both require libevent-2.1-7 (>= 2.1.8-stable). Independently ran the supplied inert cached-index fixture through the exact digest-pinned Debian base, adding --pull=never, --read-only and --network none. apt-cache policy returned candidate 999.0 from deb.torproject.org at priority 500, ahead of snapshot Debian 2.1.12-stable-8 at priority 500. A separate apt-get --simulate install --no-install-recommends libevent-2.1-7 returned exit 0 and “Inst libevent-2.1-7 (999.0 deb.torproject.org [amd64])”. The fixture does not authenticate an attacker-signed index, download a package, execute its contents, or prove a real repository compromise; it isolates APT selection after the explicitly modelled archive-signing compromise. The real ordinary-build log independently shows this library is not already present and is fetched during the new installer. No independent dependency lock or repository exclusion exists in the reviewed source. Comparing two builds against one unchanged current index does not falsify later substitution.
EVALUATED-AS: FIX — Restrict the mutable Tor repository to the exact independently hash-checked Tor package; obtain the rest of the closure only from the pinned Debian source, or verify the entire closure against independent content locks. A concrete narrow origin-exclusion probe was tested without source/file mutation: an in-container Linux memfd supplied APT preferences with “Package: tor / Pin: origin deb.torproject.org / Pin-Priority: 990”, then “Package: * / Pin: origin deb.torproject.org / Pin-Priority: -1”. In the same inert fixture, apt-cache policy then chose Debian 2.1.12-stable-8 at 500 and assigned Tor-repo 999.0 priority -1. This validates the exclusion direction, not a completed production fix or its Tor-positive path. Initial stdin-based preference injection did not apply, a host-memfd bind was rejected by runc, and the first Perl memfd invocation failed before APT; none is counted as passing evidence. The successful probe created only anonymous memory in a read-only, network-disabled container.
Closure probe: Add a spec-derived regression through the actual installer/APT selection boundary with valid pinned Tor retained, a higher-version non-Tor dependency offered by the Tor repository, and Debian's expected dependency available. Require snapshot selection or refusal before any unverified dependency installation. Include a positive case proving the exact Tor package remains selectable and its hash is checked; exercise supported architectures and existing substitution/re-fetch failures. Rebuild and rerun affected integrity/reproducibility gates. This repair need not refresh Debian dependency versions or alter runtime routing.

RT2 — Minor — The changed threat model retains an incompatible description of the repaired Tor hash boundary.
Location: THREAT-MODEL.md:170 (new update statement) and :1409 (existing §8.6 residue).
Affected property: Constitution V, honest distinction between implemented and unresolved protections.
Reachable path: An operator/auditor reading §8.6 is still told that no independent build step enforces the Tor .deb hash, whereas the new §2.6 update and installer establish that check. This is a documentation inconsistency, not an independently demonstrated runtime exploit. It also obscures the different dependency-selection residue in RT1.
Attempted falsification: Read both sections and traced Dockerfile:170 to installer:25–27; the old “enforced by no independent build step” assertion is false for the reviewed candidate's Tor archive. The live-validation and foundation gaps remain unresolved and must not be deleted as part of this correction.
EVALUATED-AS: FIX — Reconcile §8.6 with the new §2.6 entry: state the Tor artifact check and its limits, preserve T161 promotion blocking and remaining foundation/release obligations, and describe RT1 accurately until repaired. A consistency reread should find one current account of the Tor artifact's enforcement state; no runtime test is needed for this prose correction.

VERIFIED-ABSENT CHECKLIST (scoped to this 19-file candidate and affected build/torrc boundaries):
- DNS handling: no new proxy/extension resolver or DNS path in the manifest/diff. Read installer, Dockerfile and unchanged torrc; network retrieval is a build action. Whole-stack DNS leak freedom was not re-proved.
- Header propagation/payload inspection: no new negotiation/header/payload parser in the diff; only build-time shell operations are added. No request-path source was changed or cumulatively re-audited.
- Logs and metrics: new installer output contains build package/status/architecture data, not browsing input. No new application metrics/log writer is introduced. Unchanged Tor notice logging and the known T152 aggregate-policy gap are not certified absent.
- Sidecar contracts: no torrc, runtime identity, entrypoint, cookie path or healthcheck change. Supplied T032/T062/T058a logs cover named configuration/control/calibration checks. Cold T058 failures remain explicitly pending under T161; absence of a Tor isolation defect is NOT verified by those failures.
- Auth bypass: no added auth mechanism or bypass in reviewed source. Existing Tor cookie and application auth authority are unchanged; known T138 capability gaps are not closed by this review.
- Port binding/effective reachability: no new listener/publication or privilege change in the diff. USER tor and fixed UID/GID 5103 remain. Local/LAN/VPS ingress and egress compliance are not certified; T134/T155 remain owners.
- Browser extension API misuse: verified absent from this change by exact manifest/diff inventory: no extension source or permission changes. No browser execution was performed.
- Inter-plane leaks: verified absent as a new change: no DP/CP/proto/IPC source or schema edits. CP-outage and peer-auth behavior were not cumulatively tested here.
- Update channel integrity: unresolved RT1; the Tor archive itself passes independently verified signed-index/content-identity checks. No new runtime auto-update, polling or promotion action is introduced. Build helpers are removed from the runtime filesystem in source and supplied runtime inspection.
- Cover/decoy traffic: no new runtime timer, preheat or cover-traffic generator in the changed code. Native Tor maintenance remains its separately scoped traffic class; no claim of complete upstream source audit.
- Outbound connections: new APT retrieval is build-time traffic permitted by Constitution III's build/CI row. No new DP/CP runtime outbound call is added. RT1 describes the supply-chain route to malicious executable code; unchanged deployed egress capabilities were not measured.

EVIDENCE AND LIMITS:
- Reviewer: OpenAI Codex agent, GPT-6 based as identified by session instructions. Exact serving-model revision is not independently exposed. Tools actually used: functions.exec/exec_command for read-only git/source/hash inspection, Python standard library hashing/parsing, gpgv, and Docker running pinned Debian APT plus Perl for an anonymous-memory preference probe. This is an AI ticket review, not an Opus claim or external-human/anonymity-specialist review. No delegation.
- Base HEAD verified: e7c83bf4882c08ee6aa6d6a41a125263e6869a84. Serialized manifest hash matches SNAPSHOT. All 19 worktree and staged file hashes match the manifest, and staged inventory matches exactly; rechecked immediately before writing this verdict. Source remained unchanged during review. SECURITY-DECISIONS.md was hashed only. No prior reviews/findings or current codereview verdict were read.
- Read the full constitution and threat model, reviewer instructions, workflow/review-loop, contribution rules, ticket schema, scoped diff, relevant FR-011/015/016 and torrc/foundation contracts, and raw evidence. SECURITY-DECISIONS content/append-only rationale remains outside this blind review.
- Independently gpgv-verified cached InRelease against the supplied pinned-key artifact, then matched both architecture Packages hashes against the signed metadata and both actual .deb hashes against Packages and checked-in pins. Metadata Date is 2026-09-11 and Valid-Until is 2026-10-21. This checks the retained primary metadata; no fresh network fetch or upstream code audit was performed.
- Verified the hashes of all 11 log artifacts named by evidence-r1.json and inspected the relevant raw results. The supplied deterministic gate reports GREEN, 21 PASS, 1 release-reproducibility SKIP, 0 FAIL. Independent full-gate/build/live reruns were not performed in this read-only review.
- Supplied evidence retains tests-first failure and green results, a real wrong-hash build failure, native amd64 version/config/helper checks, and two cold builds with equal 96-package name/version manifests. That is not image-byte equality, closure byte comparison, or native arm64 execution. Arm64 coverage here is authenticated package identity and the supplied installer fixture only.
- T058 cold failures provide no circuit-comparison evidence. Passing T032/T062/T058a results do not erase them; T161 correctly blocks deployment/release promotion and T155/T078/T079. Candidate preparation is the reviewed scope. No deployment, public release, constitutional exception or external-human-review completion is approved by this verdict.
- Independent adversarial commands used --network none and inert cached index entries; no attacker package was fetched or executed. The later parent-authored dependency-red artifact was not needed or read for this finding. Only this verdict path was written.
